<figcaption id="mntl-figure-caption_1-0" class="comp mntl-figure-caption text-utility-100 figure-article-caption"> <span class="figure-article-caption-owner"><p>Stevica Mrdja / Getty Images</p></span>
</figcaption></figure>
<span class="heading-toc" id="toc-first-step-assess-the-situation"></span><h2 id="mntl-sc-block_5-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block health-sc-block-heading mntl-sc-block-heading"> <span class="mntl-sc-block-heading__text text-title-300"> First Step: Assess the Situation </span> </h2>
<p id="mntl-sc-block_6-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-html"> In any medical emergency, your first action should be to assess the situation. Ask yourself these questions:<span data-cite="47" class="inline-citation-node"></span>
</p>
<div id="mntl-sc-block_7-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-adslot mntl-block"></div>
<ol id="mntl-sc-block_8-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-html">
<li><strong>Is the scene safe?</strong> Assess what is going on around the injured individual. Consider whether they are safe where they are and whether you can safely approach them. Check if you have personal protective equipment (e.g., gloves) in case of blood or other bodily fluids. If they are conscious, you can ask for consent.</li>
<li><strong>Is the person responsive or unresponsive?</strong> Use the American Red Cross’s “shout-tap-shout” method to see if the person is conscious. Check if they respond, if there’s <a href="https://www.verywellhealth.com/rectal-bleeding-5271310" data-component="link" data-source="inlineLink" data-type="internalLink" data-ordinal="1">dangerous bleeding</a>, if they are breathing, and if there are any other major injuries or conditions.</li>
<li><strong>Do you need emergency medical responders (EMS)?</strong> If the person isn’t responding, can’t stay awake, isn’t breathing or is gasping, or has serious bleeding, <strong>call 911</strong>. Start CPR if cardiac arrest is possible. Find an Automated External Defibrillator (AED) if you can. Take the same actions if you suspect any life-threatening condition.</li>
<li><strong>Is the person okay without emergency medical help?</strong> The person might be fine after a minute or brief care. If they’re conscious and talking, offer more help. Ask if they have any medical conditions needing further attention. If there’s a possible head, neck, or <a href="https://www.verywellhealth.com/myelopathy-overview-4797145" data-component="link" data-source="inlineLink" data-type="internalLink" data-ordinal="2">spinal injury</a>, advise them to stay still until help arrives.</li><span class="mntl-sc-block-adslot mntl-sc-block-adslot-inline"></span>
<li><strong>Continue to provide emergency medical care</strong> based on your level of training and comfort with the situation. Call 911 if needed and if not already done. If EMS is called, remain with the individual until help arrives.</li>
</ol>
<div id="mntl-sc-block_9-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-adslot mntl-block"></div>
<div id="mntl-sc-block_10-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block health-sc-block-digiohembed mntl-block"><div id="mntl-sc-block-digiohembed_1-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block-digiohembed mntl-block"><div
id="lightbox-inline-form-d6b1ce8f-ffcf-49f7-9130-c9a0962426b2"
class="mntl-sc-block-digiohembed__container digioh-inline-unit digioh-inline-unit--sc"
data-guid="lightbox-inline-form-d6b1ce8f-ffcf-49f7-9130-c9a0962426b2"
data-title=""
>
</div></div></div>
<div id="mntl-sc-block_11-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-adslot mntl-block"></div>
<span class="heading-toc" id="toc-follow-the-abcdes-of-first-aid"></span><h2 id="mntl-sc-block_12-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block health-sc-block-heading mntl-sc-block-heading"> <span class="mntl-sc-block-heading__text text-title-300"> Follow the ABCDEs of First Aid </span> </h2>
<p id="mntl-sc-block_13-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-html"> For an <a href="https://www.verywellhealth.com/basic-first-aid-procedures-1298578" data-component="link" data-source="inlineLink" data-type="internalLink" data-ordinal="1">unconscious or unresponsive</a> person, prioritize the <strong>ABCs of first aid</strong>: Airway, Breathing, and Circulation.<span data-cite="52" class="inline-citation-node"></span>
</p>
<div id="mntl-sc-block_14-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-adslot mntl-block"></div>
<ul id="mntl-sc-block_15-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-html"> <li><strong>Airway:</strong> If someone is not breathing, you first need to open their airway, if possible.</li><li><strong>Breathing:</strong> If you’ve cleared the airway but they aren’t breathing, provide rescue breathing if you’re comfortable.</li><li><strong>Circulation: </strong>While doing rescue breathing, continue chest compressions to maintain blood circulation. Check their pulse. If there’s no pulse, start chest compressions.</li>
</ul>
<div id="mntl-sc-block_16-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-adslot mntl-block"></div>
<p id="mntl-sc-block_17-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-html"> Some first aid courses add <strong>D </strong>and <strong>E</strong>:<span data-cite="53" class="inline-citation-node"></span>
</p>
<div id="mntl-sc-block_18-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-adslot mntl-block"></div>
<ul id="mntl-sc-block_19-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-html">
<li><strong>Disability:</strong>&nbsp;This refers to conditions affecting consciousness. Check for an altered mental state, seizures, overdose, hypoglycemia, or anything impairing consciousness.</li>
<li><strong>Exposure:</strong> Remove harmful exposure if possible. Provide warm, dry clothes for <a href="https://www.verywellhealth.com/what-is-normal-body-temperature-6890498" data-component="link" data-source="inlineLink" data-type="internalLink" data-ordinal="1">hypothermia risk</a>. Note any snake bites or poisonous animals. Remove constrictive items like jewelry if needed.</li>
</ul>
<div id="mntl-sc-block_20-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-adslot mntl-block"></div>
<div id="health-sc-block-callout--explainer_1-0" class="comp health-sc-block-callout--explainer health-sc-block-callout-base mntl-sc-block-callout mntl-block theme-explainer theme-explainer" data-tracking-id="mntl-sc-block-callout" data-tracking-container="true"><h3 id="mntl-sc-block-callout-heading_2-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block-callout-heading mntl-text-block">Where to Get First Aid Training</h3>
<div id="mntl-sc-block-callout-body_2-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block-callout-body mntl-text-block expert-content"><p>Taking a formal <a href="https://www.verywellhealth.com/when-to-do-cpr-8359339" data-component="link" data-source="inlineLink" data-type="internalLink" data-ordinal="1">CPR class</a> will help you become familiar with doing chest compressions, rescue breathing, and using an AED. You can find courses from the <a href="https://www.redcross.org/take-a-class/learn-about-our-programs" data-component="link" data-source="inlineLink" data-type="externalLink" data-ordinal="2" rel="nofollow">American Red Cross</a>, your local community first responders, and online.</p></div></div>
<span class="heading-toc" id="toc-1-cardiac-arrest"></span><h2 id="mntl-sc-block_22-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block health-sc-block-heading mntl-sc-block-heading"> <span class="mntl-sc-block-heading__text text-title-300"> 1. Cardiac Arrest </span> </h2>
<figure id="health-sc-block-image--none_2-0" class="comp health-sc-block-image--none health-sc-block-image-base mntl-sc-block-image mntl-sc-block-image--no-theme no-theme mntl-sc-block theme-none figure-landscape figure-high-res"> <div class="figure-media">
<div class="img-placeholder" style="padding-bottom:66.6%;">
<img
data-src="https://www.verywellhealth.com/thmb/Q-02HI_JF3YfOtd8ZskclW3_qho=/1500x0/filters:no_upscale():max_bytes(150000):strip_icc()/GettyImages-505252032-56d1ec685f9b5879cc81553d.jpg"
width="3869"
